       "48x48": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/default/default-avatar.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Former user",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a13a248ef600739455eb",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>I don't think this is going to be as simple as configuring the servlet container to disallow deletes.  SPARQL Update is used for creating, updating and deleting triples over HTTP POST.  So Camel or any other processor would need to be able to POST in order to create audit events.</p>\n\n<p>So, I think there are three options we can consider for implementing this requirement, in order of increasing difficulty:</p>\n\n<p>1. Defer this requirement to phase 2, and assume that users who want to prevent deletion will also want to have in-repository event storage for maximum confidence that all audit records are preserved.<br/>\n2. Document how to enable access control for Sesame (to give users the ability to query the triplestore without being able to modify it) and that the Camel workflow won't delete triples.<br/>\n3. Create a SailConnectionWrapper implementation that would actually let you have a Sesame triplestore configured to allow adding statements but not deleting them (see 3. <a href=\"http://rdf4j.org/sesame/2.7/apidocs/org/openrdf/sail/helpers/SailConnectionWrapper.html\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://rdf4j.org/sesame/2.7/apidocs/org/openrdf/sail/helpers/SailConnectionWrapper.html</a>).  I don't know if this would work, or if the system would be usable in this state or not.</p>\n",
